Id: stor020i
Severity: INFO
Category: filesystem
Check: check_storage
Fix: Set Storage=none and ProcessSizeMax=0 in a file of /etc/systemd/coredump.conf.d where crashes are not debugged on the host.
References: coredump.conf(5); systemd-coredump(8); core(5); CIS Benchmarks for Linux distributions, 1.5 (core dumps restricted)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-6; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

kernel.core_pattern hands core dumps to systemd-coredump, and its
configuration keeps them: Storage= is external (the default: a file
in /var/lib/systemd/coredump, compressed, deleted after a few days by
systemd-tmpfiles) or journal (in the system journal, with the rest of
the log), and ProcessSizeMax= is above 0. A core dump is the crashed
program's memory, whatever it held: passwords, keys, the contents of
files it had open.

The finding names the Storage and ProcessSizeMax in force and the file
each comes from (coredump.conf(5): the main file first, then the
drop-ins of /etc, /run and /usr/lib's coredump.conf.d by name, the
last setting of a key winning). Storage=none keeps no dump, only the
log entry and the backtrace; ProcessSizeMax=0 stops the processing
too, and the CIS Benchmarks ask for both. It is INFO because this is
what every stock host with systemd-coredump does (Debian and Ubuntu
when it is installed, Fedora and Arch always), and because the dumps
are kept with care: a file is root's, with read access for the user
whose program crashed, and a setuid program's dump is withheld from
that user when fs.suid_dumpable is 2, which systemd's 50-coredump.conf
sets (check_sysctl's lin044i). Where nobody debugs crashes on the host,
there is no reason to keep what they leave behind:
  # mkdir -p /etc/systemd/coredump.conf.d
  # printf '[Coredump]\nStorage=none\nProcessSizeMax=0\n' > /etc/systemd/coredump.conf.d/90-none.conf

A core_pattern that is a file, not a pipe, is stor007w; a pipe to
another handler (apport, abrt) is not judged.
